<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scotch-Irish Americans</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Scots-Irish Americans</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Scotch-Irish, or Scots-Irish, are Americans descended from Presbyterian settlers from Ulster, most of them of Scottish origin, who emigrated in large numbers in the 18th century. Many landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and moved into the backcountry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> and the Carolinas, where they settled the frontier. They strongly shaped the culture of Appalachia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-south">American South</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scotch-irish-americans.jpg" alt="Scotch-Irish Americans" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lightandtruth · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Scotch-Irish_Americans_in_2023.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
